const SIDX=[["Pasta", "guides/pasta.html", "Basics History Making Types & Shapes Sauces Cooking Glossary Compiled by Kamal Kant Gautam The Pasta Guide From a handful of flour and water to hundreds of shapes — what pasta is, how it's made, where it came from, how each shape looks and feels, and which sauce belongs with it."], ["Pasta", "guides/pasta.html", "The Foundation What Is Pasta? Pasta is an unleavened dough of wheat flour and water (or eggs), shaped, then cooked in boiling water. Simple ingredients, endless variety — the shape, the flour and the sauce decide everything."], ["Pasta", "guides/pasta.html", "Durum Semolina Coarse flour from hard durum wheat. High in protein and gluten, it gives dried pasta its golden colour and firm \"al dente\" bite. Soft Wheat (00)"], ["Pasta", "guides/pasta.html", "Finely milled, silky and tender. Mixed with eggs, it makes delicate fresh pasta such as tagliatelle and filled pastas. Water or Eggs Dried pasta is usually semolina and water only. Fresh egg pasta uses roughly one egg per 100 g of flour for a rich, supple dough."], ["Pasta", "guides/pasta.html", "Other Grains Whole wheat, buckwheat, chickpea, lentil and rice flours make wholegrain and gluten-free versions. Colour can come from spinach, beetroot, tomato or squid ink."], ["Pasta", "guides/pasta.html", "Fresh Pasta (Pasta Fresca) Egg and soft flour, rolled and cut by hand or machine. Soft and tender, cooks in 2–4 minutes. Best with butter, cream and light sauces. Short shelf life."], ["Pasta", "guides/pasta.html", "Dried Pasta (Pasta Secca) Semolina and water, extruded and slow-dried. Firm, holds its shape, cooks in 8–12 minutes. Keeps for up to two years and carries robust, oily or tomato-based sauces."], ["Pasta", "guides/pasta.html", "350+ recorded shapes ~1 L water per 100 g 8–12 min dried cook time 2 yrs dried shelf life Through the Ages A Brief History Ancient roots Noodle-like doughs appear in many early cultures. Etruscan and Roman tables knew"], ["Pasta", "guides/pasta.html", "lagana , a flat baked sheet that is a distant ancestor of lasagne. ~12th century — Sicily Dried pasta is documented in Sicily, where durum wheat grows well. The geographer al-Idrisi describes strands of dough made and exported near Palermo."], ["Pasta", "guides/pasta.html", "13th–14th century Dried pasta travels by sea from ports like Genoa and Naples. Its long shelf life suits ships and trade. The Marco Polo story is a popular myth — pasta was already in Italy."], ["Pasta", "guides/pasta.html", "17th–18th century — Naples Mechanical presses and drying racks make pasta cheap and plentiful. Naples becomes the pasta capital. 19th century Tomato sauce joins pasta, creating the pairing now seen as classic. Factories scale up production."], ["Pasta", "guides/pasta.html", "20th century onward Pasta spreads worldwide with migration, with regional shapes plus wholegrain and gluten-free versions. From Wheat to Plate How Pasta Is Made"], ["Pasta", "guides/pasta.html", "Milling Durum wheat is cleaned and milled into coarse semolina. Mixing Semolina and water (or eggs) become a crumbly dough, kneaded until smooth and elastic."], ["Pasta", "guides/pasta.html", "Shaping Dough is rolled into sheets and cut, or pushed through a die. Bronze dies leave a rough surface that holds sauce; Teflon dies give a smoother finish."], ["Pasta", "guides/pasta.html", "Drying Dried pasta is dried slowly under controlled heat and humidity, from hours to a couple of days, for strength and flavour. Resting & Packing Fresh pasta is rested, cut and used or chilled quickly. Dried pasta is cooled and packed."], ["Pasta", "guides/pasta.html", "Cooking Boiled in well-salted water to al dente, then finished in the sauce with a splash of starchy pasta water. The Shape Directory Major Types of Pasta"], ["Pasta", "guides/pasta.html", "Each card shows what the pasta looks like — its shape, colour and surface — plus how to recognise it and where it is best used. Filter by family. The Golden Rule of Pairing"], ["Pasta", "guides/pasta.html", "Thin strands (spaghetti, capellini) — light oil, garlic and thin tomato sauces. Flat ribbons (fettuccine, tagliatelle, pappardelle) — cream and slow-cooked meat ragù."], ["Pasta", "guides/pasta.html", "Tubes and ridges (rigatoni, penne) — chunky sauces and bakes that fill the hollows. Twists and shells (fusilli, conchiglie) — pesto, chunky vegetables, cold salads."], ["Pasta", "guides/pasta.html", "Tiny shapes (orzo) — soups and light dishes. The Other Half Popular Sauces Explained What each sauce is, what goes into it, and how it tastes and feels — plus the shapes that suit it."], ["Pasta", "guides/pasta.html", "Good Habits Cooking Pasta Well Salt the Water About 10 g per litre. It should taste lightly of the sea — the only chance to season the pasta itself. Plenty of Water"], ["Pasta", "guides/pasta.html", "A large pot at a rolling boil so pasta moves freely. No oil needed. Al Dente Taste a minute before the packet time. Tender, with slight firmness at the centre."], ["Pasta", "guides/pasta.html", "Save Pasta Water The starchy water binds sauce to pasta. Add a ladle while tossing for a glossy finish. Finish in the Pan Move pasta into the sauce for the last minute so flavours marry. Do not rinse."], ["Pasta", "guides/pasta.html", "Serve Right Away Pasta keeps cooking and firms up fast. Plate hot, cheese and herbs at the last moment. Speak the Language Pasta Vocabulary Open the glossary (12 terms)"], ["Pasta", "guides/pasta.html", "Al dente \"To the tooth\" — cooked but still slightly firm. Semolina Coarsely ground durum wheat used for dried pasta. Durum A hard wheat rich in protein and gluten."], ["Pasta", "guides/pasta.html", "Bronze die Metal extruder plate that gives a rough, sauce-gripping surface. Pasta fresca Fresh pasta, usually made with eggs. Pasta secca Dried pasta, usually semolina and water."], ["Pasta", "guides/pasta.html", "Ragù Slow-cooked meat sauce. Pasta water Starchy cooking water used to bind and loosen sauce. Mantecare To toss vigorously with fat and starch until creamy."], ["Pasta", "guides/pasta.html", "Sfoglia A thin sheet of rolled fresh pasta dough. Rigate Ridged; the grooves hold more sauce. Lisce Smooth; best with lighter, slippery sauces. Compiled by Kamal Kant Gautam"], ["Pasta", "guides/pasta.html", "Have a query? Send your question and it will reach the author directly. Submit ← Flavour IQ"], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "The Cheese Guide Overview Milk & Origin How It's Made Directory Texture Spectrum Glossary A Field Guide The Cheese Guide Twenty cheeses, from fresh curds to aged wheels — where each one comes from, what milk shapes it, and how it's best enjoyed."], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "Cheeses profiled Milk sources Texture families 12+ Countries of origin Why It Matters More Than Just a Topping Cheese is one of humanity's oldest crafted foods — a product of patience, chemistry, and regional tradition that turns simple milk into thousands of distinct flavors and textures."], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "Flavor & Texture Ranges from mild and milky to sharp and pungent, shaped by milk, culture, and time. Culinary Versatility Melts, grates, spreads, or crumbles — adapting to almost any dish or cuisine."], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "Cultural Heritage Each cheese carries the history, geography, and tradition of the place it comes from. Nutritional Value A concentrated source of protein and calcium, valuable as part of a balanced diet."], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "Craftsmanship From curdling to aging, cheesemaking blends science with generations of hands-on skill. Universal Appeal Enjoyed everywhere — from everyday snacking to the finest dining tables."], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "The Source Milk & Origin Every cheese begins with a milk source, and that milk shapes everything downstream — fat content, flavor intensity, and which cheeses it's suited to become."], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "Most common 🐄 Cow's Milk ~3.5–4% fat · mild, sweet base The default milk for the majority of the world's cheeses — mild and versatile, taking well to both fresh and long-aged styles."], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "Mozzarella, Cheddar, Gouda Brie, Camembert, Parmesan Cream Cheese, Monterey Jack Rich & complex 🐐 Goat's Milk ~4% fat · brighter, tangier Lower in cholesterol than cow's milk, with a distinct tang; most goat cheeses are eaten fresh or lightly aged."], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "Chèvre (Goat Cheese) Some Feta blends Some Halloumi blends High fat, savory 🐑 Sheep's Milk ~6–7% fat · dense, nutty, savory Higher in fat and protein than cow's milk, giving sheep's-milk cheeses their characteristic richness and depth."], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "Feta, Manchego Roquefort Traditional Ricotta Prized, delicate 🐃 Buffalo Milk ~7–8% fat · silky, milky-sweet Higher fat content produces an especially creamy, luxurious texture — the gold standard for certain fresh cheeses."], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "Mozzarella di Bufala Paneer (regional) Burrata (occasionally) From Farm to Wheel How Cheese Is Made Nearly every cheese — fresh or aged, soft or hard — follows the same five-stage journey. What changes from one cheese to the next is the milk, the culture, the cutting, and the time."], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "Fresh cheeses Skip ripening almost entirely — eaten within days (Mozzarella, Ricotta, Cream Cheese, Paneer). Bloomy & washed rinds Ripen from the outside in over 2–6 weeks, with molds or washes actively shaping flavor (Brie, Camembert)."], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "Semi-hard & hard cheeses Pressed firmer to expel more moisture, then aged for months to years (Gouda, Cheddar, Parmesan). Blue cheeses Pierced with needles during aging so air reaches the interior, letting Penicillium mold veins develop throughout."], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "The Directory 20 Cheeses, A–Z by Family Filter by texture family to browse, or tap any card to see its origin, milk source, flavor, and best use on the menu."], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "All Cheeses Fresh & Soft Bloomy Rind Brined & Grilling Semi-Hard Hard & Aged Blue-Veined Mozzarella 🇮🇹 Italy Cow / Buffalo Texture: Soft, fresh, elastic when melted"], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "Flavor: Mild and milky, with a clean dairy sweetness Best used in: Pizzas, lasagna, pasta bakes, caprese salad, sandwiches 💡 The buffalo-milk version, mozzarella di bufala, is a protected specialty from the Campania region near Naples."], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "Burrata 🇮🇹 Italy Cow Texture: Mozzarella shell filled with cream and soft curd Flavor: Rich, luscious, buttery Best used in: Antipasti platters, caprese-style starters, drizzled with olive oil and torn into salads"], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "💡 Created in Puglia as a way to use up mozzarella scraps and cream — nothing went to waste. Ricotta 🇮🇹 Italy Cow / Sheep Whey Texture: Light, grainy-soft, spoonable"], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "Flavor: Mildly sweet, delicate Best used in: Lasagna, ravioli filling, cannoli, baked cheesecakes, desserts 💡 The name means \"recooked\" — it's traditionally made from the whey left over after making other cheeses."], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "Feta 🇬🇷 Greece Sheep / Goat Texture: Firm yet crumbly, brined Flavor: Salty and tangy Best used in: Greek salad, spanakopita, Mediterranean mezze, appetizer platters"], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "💡 Under EU law, only cheese made in Greece from sheep and goat milk can be labeled \"Feta.\" Paneer 🇮🇳 India Cow / Buffalo Texture: Firm, does not melt when cooked"], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "Flavor: Neutral, milky Best used in: Paneer tikka, palak paneer, curries, kebabs — a banquet staple 💡 Made by curdling milk with an acid (lemon juice or vinegar) rather than rennet, and pressing out the whey."], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "Cream Cheese 🇺🇸 USA Cow Texture: Soft, smooth, spreadable Flavor: Mild and creamy Best used in: Cheesecakes, bagels, canapés, frostings, dips 💡 Invented in New York in 1872; the Philadelphia brand made it a household name worldwide."], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "Mascarpone 🇮🇹 Italy Cow Texture: Ultra-smooth, thick like whipped cream Flavor: Rich and buttery Best used in: Tiramisu, dessert fillings, pasta sauces"], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "💡 Technically not a true cheese — it's made by curdling cream with acid, not rennet. Goat Cheese (Chèvre) 🇫🇷 France Goat Texture: Creamy fresh log to firmer aged wheel"], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "Flavor: Tangy, earthy Best used in: Salads, tarts, crostini, gourmet appetizers, fine dining plates 💡 Ranges widely by age — a fresh log tastes nothing like a rind-aged crottin."], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "Brie 🇫🇷 France Cow Texture: Soft with a bloomy white edible rind Flavor: Buttery, mild, slightly earthy Best used in: Cheese platters, baked brie, fine dining courses"], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "💡 Known as the \"Queen of Cheeses\" — it hails from the Brie region just east of Paris. Camembert 🇫🇷 France Cow Texture: Soft, bloomy rind, runnier when ripe"], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "Flavor: Rich, earthy, more pungent than Brie Best used in: Baked whole, cheese boards, paired with crusty bread 💡 Normandy's answer to Brie — smaller wheels, a stronger character."], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "Halloumi 🇨🇾 Cyprus Sheep / Goat Texture: Semi-hard, brined, springy and squeaky Flavor: Salty, tangy Best used in: Grilling, pan-frying, Mediterranean mezze boards, salads"], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "💡 Its unusually high melting point means it holds its shape beautifully on a hot grill. Gouda 🇳🇱 Netherlands Cow Texture: Smooth, semi-hard Flavor: Nutty, slightly sweet, caramel notes with age"], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "Best used in: Sandwiches, cheese platters, breakfast buffets 💡 Named after the Dutch city of Gouda, historically the market town where the cheese was traded."], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "Swiss (Emmental) 🇨🇭 Switzerland Cow Texture: Semi-hard with characteristic large holes (\"eyes\") Flavor: Mild, nutty Best used in: Sandwiches, burgers, fondue, gratins"], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "💡 The holes form from carbon dioxide bubbles released by bacteria during fermentation. Gruyère 🇨🇭 Switzerland Cow Texture: Dense, semi-hard to firm Flavor:"], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "Earthy, nutty, complex — more intense with age Best used in: Fondue, French onion soup, gratins, quiche 💡 A protected AOC cheese and a cornerstone ingredient of classic French cuisine."], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "Provolone 🇮🇹 Italy Cow Texture: Semi-hard, smooth Flavor: Mild (\"dolce\") to sharp (\"piccante\"), depending on age Best used in: Paninis, pizzas, grilled sandwiches"], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "💡 Sold in two classic styles — dolce (mild, aged 2–3 months) and piccante (sharp, aged much longer). Monterey Jack 🇺🇸 USA Cow Texture: Semi-hard, melts easily"], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "Flavor: Mild and buttery Best used in: Mexican dishes, nachos, burgers, casseroles 💡 Developed in Monterey, California, in the 18th century by Franciscan missionaries."], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "Manchego 🇪🇸 Spain Sheep Texture: Firm, semi-hard to hard with a distinctive zigzag rind pattern Flavor: Nutty, tangy, buttery Best used in: Tapas boards, charcuterie, paired with membrillo (quince paste)"], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "💡 Must come from Manchega sheep raised in Spain's La Mancha region to carry the name legally. Cheddar 🇬🇧 England Cow Texture: Semi-hard to hard, white or orange"], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "Flavor: Sharp and rich, intensifying with age Best used in: Burgers, sandwiches, omelets, sauces 💡 Named after the village of Cheddar in Somerset, England, where it was first made in local caves."], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "Parmesan (Parmigiano-Reggiano) 🇮🇹 Italy Cow Texture: Hard, granular, aged Flavor: Strong, nutty, deeply umami Best used in: Grated over pasta, risotto, soups, and salads"], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "💡 Aged a minimum of 12 months — premium wheels are often aged 24 to 36+ months. Blue Cheese Family 🇫🇷🇮🇹🇬🇧 France / Italy / England Sheep / Cow Texture:"], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "Crumbly to creamy, veined with mold Flavor: Sharp, tangy, pungent Best used in: Salads, dressings, steak sauces, gourmet cheese boards Key variants: Roquefort (France, sheep), Gorgonzola (Italy, cow), Stilton (England, cow)"], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "💡 The blue veins come from Penicillium mold cultures deliberately introduced during aging. At A Glance The Texture Spectrum Cheeses fall along a spectrum from fresh and unaged to hard and long-matured — a useful mental map when building a cheese board or planning a menu."], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "Speak the Language A Glossary of Cheese Terms Expand Affinage The controlled aging and ripening process that develops a cheese's final flavor and texture."], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "Bloomy Rind A soft, white, edible mold rind (e.g. Brie, Camembert) that ripens the cheese from the outside in. Brine A salt-water solution used to wash, preserve, or firm certain cheeses such as Feta and Halloumi."], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "Casein The primary milk protein that coagulates to form curd during cheesemaking. Curd The solid part of milk that separates from the whey once it coagulates — the raw material of all cheese."], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "Culture (Starter) Live bacteria added to milk to begin fermentation and develop flavor. Emmental Eyes The characteristic large holes in Swiss-style cheeses, formed by trapped carbon dioxide gas."], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "Mold-Ripened Cheese that develops flavor and texture through the action of introduced mold cultures, inside or on the rind. Pasteurization Heat-treating milk to eliminate harmful bacteria before cheesemaking."], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "PDO / AOC Protected designation labels (Protected Designation of Origin / Appellation d'Origine Contrôlée) that restrict a cheese name to a specific region and method."], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "Raw Milk Unpasteurized milk, sometimes used for artisanal cheeses to preserve natural flavor complexity. Rennet An enzyme (animal, microbial, or plant-based) used to coagulate milk into curds."], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "Rind The cheese's outer layer, which may be edible (bloomy, washed) or inedible (waxed, natural). Ripening The aging period during which flavor, aroma, and texture continue to develop after the cheese is formed."], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "Semi-Soft A texture category between fresh and semi-hard — pliable, moderate moisture, easy-melting. Terroir The combined effect of a region's soil, climate, and pasture on a cheese's character."], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "Veining The blue-green marbling in blue cheeses, produced by Penicillium mold cultures. Washed Rind A rind regularly bathed in brine, wine, or spirits during aging, producing a pungent aroma and orange hue."], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "Wheel The traditional cylindrical form in which many cheeses are aged and sold. Whey The liquid left behind after curds form — the base ingredient for cheeses like Ricotta."], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "Zigzag Rind A decorative rind pattern pressed into certain hard cheeses, such as Manchego, from traditional esparto grass molds. Compiled by Kamal Kant Gautam"], ["Cheese", "guides/cheese.html", "Have a question or request? Drop a note below — it goes directly to Kamal and no one else can see it. Name Email Your question or request Send ← Flavour IQ"], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Pastry What Is It The Kernel Flour Sugar Raising Agents Lamination Types Glossary A field guide Pastry Flour, fat, and a handful of techniques — rubbed, folded, whipped, or melted — are all that separate a hundred different pastries from each other. This guide walks through the grain, the ingredients, and the families "], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "First things first What is pastry, exactly? Pastry is both a dough and everything baked from it. Unlike bread, which gets its rise and structure mostly from yeast fermentation, pastry's character comes from how fat is worked into flour — rubbed in, folded in sheets, whipped in, or melted in. That one decision is what s"], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Flour Supplies the structure. How much gluten it develops decides whether the result is tender or chewy. Fat Coats the flour's proteins, keeping strands short and layers separate. It's what makes pastry flaky or crumbly rather than chewy."], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Technique Rubbing, folding, whipping, or melting fat into flour — the method used decides which pastry family you end up in. Where flour comes from Anatomy of a wheat kernel"], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Every flour starts as a single wheat kernel, and every kernel has three parts. Which of the three end up in the bag decides what kind of flour you're holding."], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Bran The tough outer coat, rich in fibre. Left in for whole-wheat and atta flours; sifted out for refined white flours like maida. Endosperm The bulk of the kernel — mostly starch, plus the proteins that form gluten. This is what becomes refined flour on its own."], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Germ The embryo of the seed, high in oil and nutrients. It's removed from refined flours mainly because its fat content shortens shelf life. Choosing the grind"], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Flour in pastry The only number that matters when picking a flour for pastry is its protein content — because protein is what becomes gluten once it meets water and gets worked. More gluten means more chew and more strength; less means more tenderness and shorter crumb."], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Flour Protein Typically used for Cake / pastry flour 7–9% Sweet shortcrust, choux, the most tender crumbs Maida (refined wheat) 8–10% Laminated pastry and mathri across South Asia"], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "All-purpose flour 10–12% The default for most shortcrust and general baking Whole wheat / atta 11–13% Denser, traditional doughs; includes bran and germ"], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Bread / strong flour 12–14% Doughs that need to stretch thin without tearing, like strudel Gluten-free blends n/a Rice, tapioca and starch mixes replicating pastry textures without wheat"], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "As a rule of thumb: the flakier or more laminated the pastry, the more it can tolerate — or needs — a stronger flour to hold its structure through repeated folding. The shorter and crumblier the pastry, the lower the protein should be."], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "In smaller doses What sugar actually does Pastry doesn't need sugar to work — plenty of savoury doughs have none — but where it appears, it's doing more than sweetening."], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Tenderises Sugar competes with flour for water, which slows gluten formation — one more way of keeping a dough short rather than chewy. Browns the crust"], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Two separate reactions give pastry its colour: caramelisation, where sugar alone breaks down under heat, and the Maillard reaction, where sugar and protein brown together at lower temperatures — responsible for most golden crusts."], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Holds moisture Sugar is hygroscopic — it draws in and holds onto water, which helps a baked pastry stay softer for longer. Adds flavour Beyond sweetness, sugar rounds out and carries other flavours — butter, spice, fruit — which is why even a small amount changes how a pastry tastes overall."], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Getting some lift Raising agents Pastry rises in one of three ways: a living organism producing gas, a chemical reaction producing gas, or trapped air and steam expanding under heat."], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Biological Yeast A living fungus that ferments sugars in the dough, releasing carbon dioxide slowly over time. Croissant & Danish dough Chemical Baking powder & soda"], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Reacts instantly with heat or moisture to release carbon dioxide — fast, with no fermentation time needed. Some shortcrust & quick doughs Physical Steam (lamination)"], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Folded layers of butter melt in the oven and release steam, which forces the surrounding dough sheets apart. Puff pastry & filo Physical Whipped egg foam"], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Air beaten into eggs expands under oven heat, with no added agent at all. Choux pastry Layer by layer Anatomy of a laminated pastry Slice through any laminated pastry and the folding process is written right into the crumb — dozens of thin, alternating layers, browner at the top and paler toward the base."], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Crust The glossy top surface, deeply browned by direct oven heat once all its surface steam has escaped. Puffed rim The topmost layers, which puffed and separated the most — they're closest to the heat and rise first."], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Upper flaky layers Distinct baked sheets, each one a \"leaf\" created by a single fold of butter into dough during lamination. Fold line A visible seam where two rolled sections meet — a record of one of the dough's folding turns."], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Interior layers Softer, paler layers near the centre, shielded from direct heat and holding more moisture than the crust. Lower flaky layers Thinner, denser layers near the base, compressed under the weight of the pastry above them."], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Base crust The bottom surface — paler than the top, since it sits on the tray rather than facing the oven's heat directly. The family tree Types of pastry"], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Every one of these comes back to the same two ingredients, combined differently. Tap a card for how it's made and what to look for. All Laminated Short & Crumbly"], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Choux Filo & Strudel Hot-Water & Enriched France Puff Pastry Hundreds of paper-thin layers, built by folding butter into dough again and again. Fat method"], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Butter laminated through repeated folds — no yeast involved. Texture Crisp, light, and almost entirely hollow from trapped steam. Examples Vol-au-vent, palmiers, mille-feuille."], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "What sets it apart No raising agent at all — every bit of lift comes from steam alone. England Rough Puff Pastry Puff pastry's faster cousin, with the butter left in rough chunks instead of one smooth block."], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Fat method Butter cut in and folded only a few times, less precisely than classic puff. Texture Flaky, but slightly denser and less even than true puff pastry."], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Examples Sausage rolls, jalousies, quick tarts. What sets it apart A home-kitchen shortcut that trades some precision for speed. France & Austria Croissant Dough"], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Laminated like puff pastry, but enriched with yeast, milk, and egg. Fat method Butter laminated into a yeasted, enriched dough. Texture Airy and layered, with a slight chew from the yeast."], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Examples Croissants, pain au chocolat, kouign-amann. What sets it apart The only laminated dough that rises from both fermentation and steam. Denmark, via Austria"], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Danish Dough A close relative of croissant dough, enriched further with egg yolk. Fat method Butter-laminated and yeasted, with a richer egg content than croissant dough."], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Texture Slightly denser and more cake-like. Examples Danish pastries, spandauer. What sets it apart Tightened layers that can hold a filling without collapsing."], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "France & England Shortcrust (Pâte Brisée) A crumbly, low-gluten dough made by rubbing fat into flour before any liquid is added. Fat method Butter, or a butter-lard mix, rubbed to a sandy crumb — no lamination."], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Texture Short, crumbly, and tender rather than chewy. Examples Quiche, savoury tarts, pot-pie lids. What sets it apart Mixing is kept to a minimum — \"shortening\" the gluten is the entire point."], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "France Sweet Shortcrust (Pâte Sucrée) Shortcrust's sweeter sibling, with sugar and egg yolk worked into a cookie-like dough. Fat method Butter creamed with sugar first, then flour and egg yolk added."], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Texture Firmer and more biscuit-like than plain shortcrust. Examples Fruit tart shells, linzer tarts. What sets it apart Can be pressed into a tin rather than rolled out."], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "India Mathri / Namkeen Pastry A short, flaky dough enriched with ghee, used for savoury fried or baked snacks. Fat method Ghee rubbed into flour (\"moyan\"), sometimes with a few rough folds."], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Texture Short and flaky, but sturdy enough to fry. Examples Mathri, samosa pastry, kachori dough. What sets it apart Built for frying rather than baking, so it's firmer than European shortcrust."], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "France Pâte à Choux A cooked dough, unique for having no lamination or rubbing-in at all. Fat method Butter melted into water, flour stirred in over heat, then eggs beaten in off the heat."], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Texture Light and hollow, almost custardy inside once baked. Examples Éclairs, profiteroles, gougères, churros. What sets it apart Cooked twice — once on the stove, once in the oven — and rises on egg and steam alone."], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Greece, Turkey & beyond Filo / Phyllo Dough stretched or rolled until it's tissue-paper thin, then layered with fat between the sheets. Fat method Brushed between stacked raw sheets — butter or oil — rather than folded in."], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Texture Very crisp, with layers that shatter. Examples Baklava, spanakopita, börek. What sets it apart Layers are stacked, not folded — each sheet is rolled thin on its own first."], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Austria & Germany Strudel Dough A stretched dough, pulled by hand until it's almost see-through. Fat method Little to none in the dough itself; brushed with melted butter as it's rolled with its filling."], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Texture Thin and crisp outside, with a soft filling inside. Examples Apfelstrudel. What sets it apart Stretched, not rolled — it needs a strong gluten network to get this thin without tearing."], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "England Hot-Water Crust A sturdy dough made by mixing flour with fat melted into boiling water. Fat method Lard (or butter) melted into hot water, then mixed into flour while still hot."], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Texture Firm and mouldable while warm, hardening as it cools. Examples Pork pies, game pies. What sets it apart Shaped by hand around a mould while still warm, rather than rolled cold."], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "England Suet Pastry A soft dough made with shredded beef or vegetable suet instead of a fat rubbed in cold. Fat method Suet stays in small solid pieces through steaming, rather than melting like butter."], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Texture Soft, spongy, and absorbent. Examples Steamed puddings, dumplings. What sets it apart Usually steamed or boiled rather than baked, so the fat never fully melts in."], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Spain & Latin America Empanada Dough A firm, rollable dough, sometimes with a splash of wine or vinegar for extra crispness. Fat method Butter or lard worked into flour along with egg."], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Texture Sturdy enough to hold a wet filling; crisp when fried or baked. Examples Empanadas. What sets it apart Built to seal completely around a filling and hold its shape either way it's cooked."], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "One more thing Glossary The terms that came up throughout this guide, gathered in one place. All-Purpose Flour Flour milled to a moderate protein content, used as the general default across most shortcrust and everyday baking."], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Atta Whole wheat flour common across South Asia, milled finer than Western whole wheat and including the bran and germ. Baking Powder A chemical raising agent that already contains its own acid, reacting with moisture and heat to release carbon dioxide."], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Baking Soda A chemical raising agent that needs an acidic ingredient elsewhere in the dough to react and release carbon dioxide. Beurrage The block of butter folded into dough during lamination — the French term for the \"butter layer.\""], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Bran The tough outer coat of a wheat kernel, rich in fibre and left in for whole-wheat and atta flours. Caramelisation The browning that happens when sugar alone breaks down under heat, without any protein involved."], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Choux A cooked pastry dough that relies on steam and egg for its rise, rather than any yeast or chemical agent. Creaming Beating fat and sugar together until light and aerated — the starting step for many enriched doughs."], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Detrempe The flour-and-water dough that butter is laminated into — the French term for the \"dough layer.\" Docking Pricking small holes into rolled-out pastry before baking, so steam can escape and the pastry doesn't puff unevenly."], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Endosperm The starchy bulk of a wheat kernel, containing the proteins that become gluten. Germ The nutrient-rich embryo of a wheat kernel, usually removed from refined flour."], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Gluten The elastic protein network formed when a wheat flour's proteins are hydrated and worked. How much forms decides chew versus tenderness. Hygroscopic"], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Able to draw in and hold onto moisture from the air — a property of sugar that helps baked pastry stay soft for longer. Lamination The process of folding a fat repeatedly through a dough to build up distinct, separate layers."], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Maida A finely milled, refined wheat flour common across South Asia, similar to Western all-purpose or cake flour. Maillard Reaction The browning reaction between sugars and proteins at moderate heat, responsible for most of pastry's golden crust colour."], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Moyan Ghee or fat rubbed into flour before liquid is added — the South Asian equivalent of Western \"rubbing in.\" Rubbing In Working cold fat into flour with the fingertips until it resembles breadcrumbs, coating the flour to limit gluten formation."], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Shortening Any fat used to \"shorten\" gluten strands, making a dough tender rather than chewy; also the name for solid vegetable fats sold for this purpose."], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Turn A single fold-and-roll step in laminating a dough. Most laminated pastries go through three to six turns. Viennoiserie The category of enriched, laminated, yeasted doughs that sit between bread and pastry, such as croissants and Danish."], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Yeast A living organism that ferments sugars in dough, releasing carbon dioxide slowly as it rises. Pastry Compiled by Kamal Kant Gautam Have a question?"], ["Pastry", "guides/pastry.html", "Ask anything about pastry, or send a note — it'll come straight through. Name Email Question / message Send ← Flavour IQ"], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "🍵 The World of Tea What Is Tea Processing True Teas Herbal Teas World Culture Health Brewing Guide Storage Glossary A COMPLETE FIELD GUIDE The Complete World of Tea"], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Everything worth knowing about tea in one place — where each style comes from, how it's made, how to brew it properly, and what it actually does for you. Tap any card to explore, or use the filters to narrow things down."], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "☕ Compiled by Kamal Kant Gautam THE BASICS One plant, almost every tea you've had Green, yellow, white, oolong, black and dark (including pu-erh) all come from the same evergreen shrub,"], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Camellia sinensis . Nothing about the plant changes between them — what changes is how the leaf is handled the moment it's picked: how much it withers, how it's shaped, and above all, how much it's allowed to oxidise before the process is stopped. That single variable — oxidation — is the entire reason tea comes in so "], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "2 varieties Camellia sinensis var. sinensis (China, smaller leaf) and var. assamica (Assam, larger leaf, bolder flavour) Not tea at all Herbal \"teas\" are tisanes — infusions of herbs, flowers, fruit or root with no Camellia sinensis in them"], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "~5,000 years of continuous cultivation and drinking history, longer than almost any beverage besides water 2nd most-drunk beverage in the world after water, ahead of coffee"], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "FROM BUSH TO CUP How tea is actually made Six broad steps turn a fresh leaf into every style on this page — how far it goes through them, and in what order, decides which category it lands in."], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "1. Pluck Two leaves and a bud, hand-picked at the shoot tip. 2. Wither Leaves lose moisture, going soft and pliable. 3. Roll / Shape Bruises the leaf, releasing juices and enzymes."], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "4. Oxidise Open air turns leaf enzymes darker and stronger — stopped early for green, longer for black. 5. Fix / Dry Heat locks in the flavour and halts oxidation for good."], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "6. Sort & Grade Leaves are graded by size and quality before packing. THE SIX CLASSIC CATEGORIES The true teas, by oxidation level Ordered from least to most processed — the traditional Chinese classification that the rest of the world's tea names still map onto."], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Tap a card to open it. Use the filters to narrow down by oxidation level. All 6 Unoxidised Lightly oxidised Semi-oxidised Fully oxidised Fermented White Tea"], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Minimally processed Origin: Fujian, China — Fuding & Zhenghe counties Oxidation Tap to see history, brewing & benefits → History Prized at the Song dynasty imperial court a thousand years ago; the modern Silver Needle style was refined for export trade in the 1800s."], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Notable styles: Silver Needle (Bai Hao Yinzhen), White Peony (Bai Mudan) Brewing 75–85°C · 2g per 200ml · steep 4–5 min Benefits Highest antioxidant retention of any tea due to minimal processing"], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Gentle, low caffeine Know this: made only from young buds and first leaves, still coated in fine silvery down. Yellow Tea Lightly, slowly oxidised Origin:"], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Hunan & Sichuan, China — Junshan Island, Mengding Mountain Oxidation Tap to see history, brewing & benefits → History A rare imperial tribute tea through the Ming and Qing dynasties. Its extra \"sealing yellow\" step is slow and labour-intensive, which kept it niche even in China."], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Notable styles: Junshan Yinzhen, Huoshan Huangya Brewing 75–85°C · 2g per 200ml · steep 2–3 min Benefits Similar antioxidants to green tea, milder on the stomach"], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Smoother, less grassy than green Know this: leaves rest wrapped in cloth or paper after firing, mellowing the flavour without full oxidation — one of the rarest tea styles left."], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Green Tea Unoxidised Origin: China (Zhejiang, Fujian) & Japan (Uji, Shizuoka) Oxidation Tap to see history, brewing & benefits → History Legend credits Emperor Shennong in 2737 BCE. Buddhist monks carried it to Japan by the 9th century, where it evolved into matcha and the tea ceremony."], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Notable styles: Dragon Well (Longjing), Sencha, Matcha, Gunpowder Brewing 70–80°C · 2g per 200ml · steep 2–3 min Benefits Rich in EGCG catechins, a potent antioxidant"], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "L-theanine gives calm, focused energy Know this: never use boiling water — it scorches the leaf and turns the cup bitter and grassy. Oolong Tea Semi-oxidised (10–80%)"], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Origin: Fujian & Guangdong, China; Taiwan's high mountains Oxidation Tap to see history, brewing & benefits → History Named \"black dragon,\" it emerged in the Wuyi Mountains under the Qing dynasty. Taiwanese growers spent the 19th–20th centuries refining high-mountain versions."], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Notable styles: Tie Guan Yin, Da Hong Pao, Dong Ding, Taiwanese High Mountain Brewing 85–95°C · 3g per 150ml · short 3–5 min infusions, re-steeped Benefits"], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Metabolism-supporting compounds A calmer lift than coffee or black tea Know this: oxidation ranges from 10–80%, so flavour swings from light and floral to dark and roasted."], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Black Tea Fully oxidised Origin: China (Wuyi), India (Assam, Darjeeling), Sri Lanka, Kenya Oxidation Tap to see history, brewing & benefits → History Developed in Fujian's Wuyi Mountains in the 1600s. The British East India Company's taste for it drove colonial-era plantations across Assam from the 1830s."], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Notable styles: Assam, Darjeeling, Ceylon, Keemun, Lapsang Souchong Brewing 95–100°C · 2.5g per 200ml · steep 3–5 min Benefits Highest caffeine of the true teas"], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Theaflavins linked to heart health Know this: the base of most blends — English Breakfast, Earl Grey and classic chai all start here. Dark Tea (Pu-erh)"], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Post-fermented & aged Origin: Yunnan, China Process Fermented & aged, months to decades Tap to see history, brewing & benefits → History Compressed into cakes for the Ancient Tea Horse Road since the Tang dynasty, so it could survive long journeys by mule — ageing was later prized much like wine."], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Notable styles: Sheng (raw, ages naturally), Shou (ripe, fast-fermented), Liu Bao Brewing 95–100°C · rinse leaves first, then 3–5 min, re-steeps many times"], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Benefits Traditionally used to aid digestion May support healthy cholesterol levels Know this: two styles — raw sheng , which ages naturally over years, and ripe"], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "shou , fermented quickly by humid piling. TISANES Herbal teas — not tea, but every bit as worth knowing Camellia sinensis in any of these — just herbs, flowers, fruit or root, brewed the same simple way. All are naturally caffeine-free."], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Tap a card to open it. Filter by what you're looking for. All 8 Relaxation Digestion Antioxidant Chamomile Matricaria chamomilla Origin: Egypt & Europe"], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Tap for brewing & benefits → History An ancient Egyptian medicinal herb, valued for centuries as a calming remedy. Brewing 90–95°C · steep 5–7 min Flavour & caffeine"], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Sweet, floral · caffeine-free Benefits Relaxation and better sleep Soothes digestion Service tip: best offered after dinner; pairs well with honey. Peppermint"], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Mentha × piperita Origin: Europe & the Middle East Tap for brewing & benefits → History Used since Greek and Roman times as a digestive aid. Brewing 95°C · steep 5–7 min"], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Flavour & caffeine Cool, refreshing · caffeine-free Benefits Aids digestion Relieves bloating Service tip: an excellent post-meal digestive tea. Hibiscus"], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Hibiscus sabdariffa Origin: Africa Tap for brewing & benefits → History A traditional drink in Egypt and Sudan for generations. Brewing 95°C · steep 5–8 min"], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Flavour & caffeine Tart, cranberry-like · caffeine-free Benefits Rich in vitamin C High in antioxidants Service tip: serve iced as a refreshing welcome drink."], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Rooibos Aspalathus linearis Origin: South Africa Tap for brewing & benefits → History An indigenous Khoisan herbal drink, harvested for generations in the Cederberg region."], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Brewing 95–100°C · steep 5–10 min Flavour & caffeine Nutty, naturally sweet · caffeine-free Benefits Antioxidants Gentle on the stomach Service tip: ideal caffeine-free stand-in for black tea."], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Lemongrass Cymbopogon citratus Origin: India & Southeast Asia Tap for brewing & benefits → History A staple Ayurvedic herb, used for its cooling, citrus qualities."], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Brewing 95°C · steep 5–7 min Flavour & caffeine Citrusy, light · caffeine-free Benefits Supports digestion Calming effect Service tip: works well on wellness and spa menus."], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Ginger Zingiber officinale Origin: South Asia Tap for brewing & benefits → History Central to Ayurveda and Traditional Chinese Medicine for millennia."], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Brewing 95–100°C · steep 7–10 min Flavour & caffeine Spicy, warming · caffeine-free Benefits Eases nausea and aids digestion Supports immunity Service tip:"], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "popular in monsoon and winter as a warming drink. Lavender Lavandula angustifolia Origin: Mediterranean, chiefly Provence, France Tap for brewing & benefits →"], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "History Used since Roman times for bathing, perfumery, and calming remedies. Brewing 90–95°C · steep 4–5 min Flavour & caffeine Floral, slightly sweet · caffeine-free"], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Benefits May ease anxiety and tension headaches Supports restful sleep Service tip: blends beautifully with chamomile for a bedtime pot. Turmeric Curcuma longa"], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Origin: South Asia, primarily India Tap for brewing & benefits → History Central to Ayurveda for thousands of years, used as both medicine and dye. Brewing"], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Simmer 8–10 min, often with black pepper & ginger for absorption Flavour & caffeine Earthy, warm, peppery · caffeine-free Benefits Curcumin has anti-inflammatory properties"], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Traditionally used for joint comfort Service tip: popular as \"golden milk\" — simmered with milk and honey. RITUAL & SERVICE Tea around the world The same leaf, shaped into six very different rituals — worth knowing for anyone serving guests from these traditions."], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "China — Gongfu Cha Small clay Yixing pots and many short, deliberate infusions, treating tea appreciation as a slow, attentive ritual rather than a quick drink."], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Japan — Chanoyu Matcha whisked to a froth in a ceremony rooted in wabi-sabi — imperfection, stillness, and quiet hospitality practised in dedicated tea houses."], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Britain — Afternoon Tea Black tea with milk, plus scones and finger sandwiches — a tradition credited to the Duchess of Bedford in the 1840s to bridge the gap to a late dinner."], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "India — Masala Chai Black tea boiled with milk, sugar, and warming spices — cardamom, ginger, cloves, cinnamon — sold fresh by chai-wallahs on nearly every street corner."], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Morocco — Atay (Mint Tea) Green tea with fresh mint and sugar, poured from height into small glasses to build a foam — a ritual gesture of hospitality offered to every guest."], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Russia — Samovar Tea A strong concentrate (zavarka) brewed separately and diluted to taste with hot water from a samovar, often sipped with jam or a sugar cube."], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "WHAT'S ACTUALLY IN THE CUP Tea's key compounds Most of what tea is known for traces back to a handful of naturally occurring compounds — present in different amounts depending on how the leaf was processed."], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Polyphenols & catechins Plant antioxidants, most concentrated in unoxidised teas. EGCG, the standout catechin in green tea, is one of the most studied antioxidants in the plant kingdom."], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Theaflavins & thearubigins Formed as catechins oxidise during black tea processing — they give black tea its colour and are linked to cardiovascular benefits."], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "L-theanine An amino acid found almost exclusively in tea. It promotes calm, focused alertness and takes the edge off caffeine's jitteriness. Caffeine Naturally occurring stimulant; the amount varies by tea type, leaf grade, water temperature, and steep time — not just by category."], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Relative caffeine per cup (approximate) White Low Green Low–med Yellow Low–med Oolong Medium Pu-erh Medium Black High Coffee Highest Herbal None Figures are approximate and shown for reference only — actual caffeine depends heavily on leaf grade, water temperature and steep time."], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "GET IT RIGHT Brewing guide, at a glance Water temperature is the single most common mistake — too hot for a delicate tea scorches it; too cool for a robust one under-extracts it."], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Type Oxidation Water Temp Leaf Amount Steep Time Re-steeping White Minimal 75–85°C 2g / 200ml 4–5 min 1–2 times Yellow Light 75–85°C 2g / 200ml 2–3 min"], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "1–2 times Green None 70–80°C 2g / 200ml 2–3 min 2–3 times Oolong 10–80% 85–95°C 3g / 150ml 3–5 min 3–5+ times Black Full 95–100°C 2.5g / 200ml 3–5 min"], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "1–2 times Dark / Pu-erh Fermented 95–100°C 3g / 150ml rinse, then 3–5 min 5+ times Herbal / Tisane N/A 90–100°C 1.5–2g / 200ml 5–10 min Rarely KEEP IT FRESH"], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Storage & serving notes Air, light, heat, moisture The four enemies of tea. Store leaves airtight, away from sunlight and away from strong-smelling foods or spices, which tea absorbs readily."], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Use it within its window Green and white teas taste best within 6–12 months of production; black and oolong hold up longer. Pu-erh is the exception — it's meant to age for years."], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Water quality matters Heavily chlorinated or very hard water flattens flavour — filtered water gives a noticeably cleaner cup. Bitterness is usually a brewing fault"], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Over-steeping or over-heating the water is the most common cause of a bitter cup, not the tea itself. SPEAK THE LANGUAGE Glossary of tea terms Tap a term to reveal its definition."], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Oxidation The chemical reaction between leaf enzymes and oxygen that darkens the leaf and deepens flavour — the main axis separating tea types. Flush A harvest period. Darjeeling's \"first flush\" (spring) is light and floral; the \"second flush\" (summer) is fuller and muscatel."], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Terroir How soil, altitude and climate at a specific origin shape a tea's character — the same idea used for wine. Orthodox vs. CTC Orthodox tea is rolled whole-leaf by hand or gentle machine; CTC (Crush-Tear-Curl) is cut into small uniform pellets for strong, fast-brewing tea, common in chai."], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Single-origin Tea from one estate or garden, kept unblended, so its specific terroir and character come through. Tisane The correct term for any herbal infusion that contains no Camellia sinensis — technically not \"tea\" at all."], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Liquor The tea industry's term for the brewed liquid itself — its colour, clarity and taste. Astringency The dry, puckering sensation from tannins, especially in over-steeped or high-temperature brewed tea."], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Umami A savoury depth found especially in shade-grown Japanese greens like gyokuro and matcha. Flowering tea Hand-tied bundles of tea leaves and flowers that unfurl into a bloom shape when steeped in hot water."], ["Tea", "guides/tea.html", "Compiled by Kamal Kant Gautam A complete reference guide to the world of tea — true teas, herbal tisanes, brewing, culture and care. ← Flavour IQ"], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "☕ The World of Coffee What Is Coffee Bean Anatomy Processing Roast Levels Bean Types Brewing Methods World Culture Geography Health Decaf Storage Glossary"], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "A COMPLETE FIELD GUIDE The Complete World of Coffee From cherry to cup — where coffee comes from, how it's processed and roasted, every major brewing method explained, and what geography does to the flavour in your mug. Tap any card to explore, or filter to narrow things down."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "☕ Compiled by Kamal Kant Gautam THE BASICS One fruit, four species, an entire world of flavour Coffee starts life as a cherry on an evergreen shrub of the genus"], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Coffea . Inside each cherry sit two seeds — the \"beans\" — that are removed, dried, roasted and ground to make the drink. Only a handful of the roughly 130 known Coffea species are grown commercially, and just two, Arabica and Robusta, account for almost everything the world drinks."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Legend of Kaldi A 9th-century Ethiopian goat herder said to have discovered coffee after his goats grew energetic from eating cherries The Bean Belt Commercial coffee grows only in the tropics, roughly between the Tropic of Cancer and Tropic of Capricorn"], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "2 dominant species Arabica (~60–70% of world production) and Robusta (~30–40%) make up almost the entire market One of the world's most widely traded agricultural commodities, grown across more than 70 countries"], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "INSIDE THE CHERRY Anatomy of a coffee bean What you grind and brew is actually the seed at the centre of a small stone fruit — every layer removed along the way has a name and a job."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Whole cherry Green bean Roasted bean Outer Skin (Exocarp) The cherry's thin outer peel — green when unripe, turning red or yellow as it matures. Pulp (Mesocarp)"], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "The sweet, fleshy fruit layer beneath the skin, removed early in processing. Mucilage A sticky, sugar-rich layer that clings to the parchment — key to fermentation and honey-processed coffees."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Parchment (Endocarp) A papery protective hull, nicknamed \"pergamino,\" removed by milling after drying. Silver Skin (Spermoderm) A thin membrane hugging the bean itself — the papery \"chaff\" that flakes off during roasting."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Bean (Endosperm) The seed itself. Two flat-sided beans usually sit face to face inside one cherry. Peaberry: occasionally only one seed develops inside the cherry, growing round instead of flat-sided. Some roasters sort these out and sell them as a specialty grade."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "FROM CHERRY TO GREEN BEAN Harvesting, processing, sorting & grading Seven stages turn a ripe cherry into the roasted, ground coffee in your cup — including the processing choice that shapes flavour more than almost anything else."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "1. Harvest Ripe red cherries hand-picked or strip-picked from the branch. 2. Process Washed, natural, or honey method — separates fruit from seed. 3. Dry"], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Sun-dried on raised beds or patios to ~11% moisture. 4. Mill / Hull Parchment and silver skin removed, revealing green coffee. 5. Sort & Grade By size, density and defects — then scored by cupping."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "6. Roast Heat transforms green beans — light to dark, aroma and colour develop. 7. Grind & Brew Grind size and method decided by how you're brewing. HEAT CHANGES EVERYTHING"], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Roast levels The same green bean can become three very different coffees, purely from how long and how hot it's roasted. Same bean, different roast — colour deepens as sugars caramelise and oils migrate outward."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Light Roast ~180–205°C internal temp Color: Light brown, dry surface, no oil sheen Tap for details → Flavour Bright, fruity, floral, tea-like — origin character comes through strongly, with higher perceived acidity."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Common names Cinnamon roast, City roast Best for Pour-over, Chemex, AeroPress — methods that highlight clarity and nuance Know this: favoured by the \"third wave\" specialty scene, especially in Nordic-style filter coffee."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Medium Roast ~210–220°C internal temp Color: Medium brown, occasional light sheen Tap for details → Flavour Balanced acidity and body, notes of caramel and nuts — the classic \"all-purpose\" roast."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Common names City roast, Breakfast blend, American roast Best for Drip machines, French press, everyday home brewing Know this: the most common roast level sold in supermarkets worldwide."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Dark Roast ~225–240°C+ internal temp Color: Dark brown to near-black, visible oil sheen Tap for details → Flavour Bold, smoky, bittersweet, heavy body — much of the origin's own character is masked by the roast itself."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Common names Full City, French roast, Italian/Espresso roast Best for Espresso, Moka pot, Turkish coffee Know this: darker roasts actually have slightly less caffeine by volume than lighter roasts of the same weight, since beans lose mass and expand as they roast."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "NOT ALL COFFEE IS THE SAME PLANT The four commercial coffee species Almost everything you drink comes from just two of these — the other two are niche, but worth knowing."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Arabica Coffea arabica Origin: Ethiopian highlands Tap for details → Growing conditions High altitude (600–2000m), cooler temperatures — more delicate, more susceptible to pests, frost and disease."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Caffeine & flavour Lower caffeine (~1.2–1.5%); smoother, more complex, aromatic, higher acidity Share of world production ~60–70% Know this: major producers include Brazil, Colombia, Ethiopia and much of Central America."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Robusta Coffea canephora Origin: Congo basin, Central/West Sub-Saharan Africa Tap for details → Growing conditions Lower altitude (200–800m), hotter climates — hardier, more disease-resistant, higher yields."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Caffeine & flavour Higher caffeine (~2.2–2.7%); bolder, more bitter, earthy/woody, produces thicker crema Share of world production ~30–40% Know this:"], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "the backbone of espresso blends (for crema) and most instant coffee, thanks to cost and higher solubility. Major producers: Vietnam, Brazil, Uganda. Liberica"], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Coffea liberica Origin: West Africa (Liberia); grown in the Philippines & Malaysia Tap for details → Growing conditions Tolerates heat and humidity better than Arabica; large, irregularly shaped beans."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Caffeine & flavour Distinct floral, fruity, sometimes smoky or woody aroma unlike either Arabica or Robusta. Share of world production Under 2% Know this:"], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "known locally as \"Barako\" in the Philippines, where it has a small but devoted following. Excelsa Coffea excelsa (a Liberica variant) Origin: Southeast Asia"], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Tap for details → Growing conditions Hardy, drought-tolerant tree; now classified by many botanists as a variety of Liberica rather than its own species."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Caffeine & flavour Tart and fruity with a complex profile that blends light-roast brightness with dark-roast depth. Share of world production Very small — mostly used to add complexity to blends"], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Know this: grown commercially in Vietnam, the Philippines and parts of India. TYPES OF PREPARATION Brewing methods, from espresso to instant Same beans, wildly different cups — grind size, water temperature, pressure and time are the four levers every method plays with differently."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Tap a card to open it. Filter by how it's brewed. All 11 Manual / Pour Pressure-based Immersion No brewing needed Espresso Italy, early 1900s Invented by:"], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Luigi Bezzera (1901), refined by Achille Gaggia (1940s) Tap for method & flavour → Method Fine grind · 18–20g in, 36–40g out (1:2) · 9 bar pressure · 90–96°C · 25–30 sec"], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Flavour Concentrated, syrupy, with a golden-brown crema layer on top. Fun fact: crema — the foam on top — only forms under real pressure, which is what separates true espresso from strong drip coffee."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "French Press France / Italy, patented 1929 Also called: Press pot, cafetière Tap for method & flavour → Method Coarse grind · 1:15 ratio · steep 4 min · plunge slowly · full immersion"], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Flavour Full-bodied and rich — the metal mesh lets natural oils through, unlike paper filters. Fun fact: because it's unfiltered, French press coffee carries more of the oily compounds that paper filters usually trap."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Pour-Over (V60) Paper filter: Germany 1908 · Cone: Japan, 1980s Also called: Hario V60, Kalita Wave Tap for method & flavour → Method Medium-fine grind · 1:16 ratio · 30 sec bloom, then slow spiral pours · 2.5–3.5 min total · 92–96°C"], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Flavour Clean, bright, highlights subtle origin notes. Fun fact: Melitta Bentz, a German housewife, invented the paper coffee filter in 1908 using blotting paper from her son's schoolbook."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Chemex USA, invented 1941 Invented by: Peter Schlumbohm, a German chemist Tap for method & flavour → Method Medium-coarse grind · 1:15–1:17 ratio · thick proprietary paper filter · 4–5 min total"], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Flavour Exceptionally clean and light-bodied — the thick filter removes almost all oils and sediment. Fun fact: the Chemex's hourglass design is part of the Museum of Modern Art's permanent collection."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "AeroPress USA, invented 2005 Invented by: Alan Adler (also invented the Aerobie flying disc) Tap for method & flavour → Method Medium-fine grind · steep 1–2 min · press ~30 sec · 80–85°C · many recipe variations"], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Flavour Clean like a pour-over but with fuller body; extremely versatile. Fun fact: it's popular enough to have its own annual World AeroPress Championship."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Moka Pot Italy, invented 1933 Invented by: Luigi De Ponti, for Alfonso Bialetti Tap for method & flavour → Method Fine-medium grind · steam pressure pushes water up through the grounds into the top chamber"], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Flavour Strong and bold — semi-espresso character, though with less crema and pressure than a true machine. Fun fact: its iconic octagonal aluminium shape hasn't changed since the 1930s and sits in nearly every Italian kitchen."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Cold Brew Roots in Kyoto-style drip; popularised in the US, 2000s–2010s Style: Steeped cold, no heat at all Tap for method & flavour → Method Coarse grind · steeped in room-temp or cold water 12–24 hrs · filtered · served over ice or diluted"], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Flavour Smooth, naturally sweeter, low acidity, less bitter than hot-brewed coffee. Fun fact: cold brew concentrate keeps well refrigerated for up to two weeks."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Siphon / Vacuum Pot Germany/France, 1830s–40s; refined in Japan Also called: Vacuum pot, syphon Tap for method & flavour → Method Vapor pressure pushes water up to meet the grounds; removing the heat pulls the brew back down through a filter"], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Flavour Clean, aromatic, tea-like clarity with a fuller body than pour-over. Fun fact: as much theatre as brewing — often performed tableside in Japanese kissaten cafés."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Turkish Coffee Ottoman Empire, 16th century Vessel: Ibrik / cezve — small long-handled copper pot Tap for method & flavour → Method Extremely fine, powder-like grind · simmered slowly with water (often sugar) · brought to foam multiple times · served unfiltered"], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Flavour Intense and thick, often spiced with cardamom; grounds settle at the bottom of the cup. Fun fact: recognised by UNESCO as an Intangible Cultural Heritage tradition in Turkey."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Drip Machine USA, popularised from the 1970s Also called: Auto-drip, filter coffee maker Tap for method & flavour → Method Medium grind · 1:16–1:18 ratio · machine drips hot water evenly over grounds into a carafe"], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Flavour Consistent and mild — built for convenience over nuance. Fun fact: the most common way coffee is brewed at home across the US and much of the Western world."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Instant Coffee Pre-brewed, then dehydrated Made via: Spray-drying or freeze-drying brewed coffee into powder or granules Tap for details & comparison →"], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Method Just add hot water — no brewing equipment needed Flavour Milder and flatter than fresh-brewed, but fast, consistent, and shelf-stable for years."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Fun fact: usually made from Robusta beans, chosen for cost efficiency and how readily they dissolve. Instant vs. fresh brewed, at a glance Feature Instant Coffee"], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Fresh Brewed Processing Brewed, then spray- or freeze-dried into powder Ground beans brewed fresh, per cup Prep time Seconds 2–10 minutes depending on method"], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Taste Milder, flatter, less aromatic Full aroma and origin character Shelf life 1–2+ years, unopened Best within 2–4 weeks of roast (whole bean) Typical bean"], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Usually Robusta Arabica or Robusta, either RITUAL & SERVICE Coffee around the world The same bean, shaped into six very different rituals — each with its own etiquette worth knowing."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Italy — Espresso Bar Quick espresso taken standing \"al banco\" at the bar; cappuccino is traditionally an morning-only order, never after a meal. Ethiopia — Coffee Ceremony"], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Green beans roasted fresh in a pan before guests, ground by hand, and brewed in a clay jebena — served across three ceremonial rounds. Turkey — Turkish Coffee"], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Brewed in an ibrik, served with a glass of water and Turkish delight — the leftover grounds are traditionally read for fortune-telling. Vietnam — Cà Phê Sữa Đá"], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Strong robusta drips slowly through a small metal phin filter over sweetened condensed milk, then poured over ice. Japan — Precision Brewing Pour-over and siphon methods refined to near-ritual precision, alongside a ubiquitous culture of canned and bottled coffee vending machines."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Brazil — Cafezinho The world's largest coffee producer for over 150 years; a small, sweet, strong \"cafezinho\" is a routine gesture of hospitality. South India — Filter Coffee"], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Introduced via Chikmagalur in the 1600s, when legend says the pilgrim Baba Budan smuggled seven coffee seeds out of Yemen. A metal filter drips a strong, dark-roast decoction — often blended with chicory — which is then mixed with hot milk and sugar, and poured back and forth between tumbler and davara from a height to"], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "TERROIR MATTERS Geographical impact on coffee Altitude, latitude and soil shape flavour as much as processing does — the same idea winemakers call terroir."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "THE BEAN BELT ~25°N to 30°S Americas Brazil, Colombia, Central America Moderate altitude, volcanic soils in parts — balanced, nutty, chocolatey cups with medium acidity. Brazil alone grows roughly a third of the world's coffee."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Africa & Arabia Ethiopia, Kenya, Yemen Coffee's birthplace region — high altitude and heirloom varieties give bright, floral, fruity, wine-like acidity prized by specialty roasters."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Asia-Pacific Indonesia, Vietnam, Papua New Guinea Humid climates and wet-hulling processing common in the region produce earthy, full-bodied, low-acidity coffees with spicy, herbal notes."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "WHAT'S ACTUALLY IN THE CUP Coffee's key compounds Most of coffee's effects trace back to a handful of naturally occurring compounds, in amounts that shift with roast level and brewing method."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Caffeine Coffee's signature stimulant. Brewing method affects it as much as bean type — longer contact time and no filter both tend to raise it. Chlorogenic acids"], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Plant antioxidants found in green coffee, partly broken down by roasting — lighter roasts generally retain more. Trigonelline Contributes to coffee's aroma and breaks down during roasting into niacin (vitamin B3)."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Diterpenes (cafestol & kahweol) Found in unfiltered coffee like French press and Turkish coffee; paper filters (pour-over, drip) trap most of these compounds."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Relative caffeine per standard serving (approximate) Decaf Minimal Instant Low–med Espresso (1 shot) Medium* Drip / Filter High French Press High Cold Brew"], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Highest *Espresso is concentrated into a much smaller volume — per ounce it's the strongest on this list, but a standard shot is only ~1oz versus a full cup of the others. Figures are approximate."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "CAFFEINE, MOSTLY REMOVED Understanding decaf Decaffeinated coffee is regular coffee with roughly 97% or more of its caffeine removed — always done to the green bean, before roasting, using one of three main processes."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Tap a process to see how it works. Swiss Water Process Chemical-free, water only Uses: Hot water and a green coffee extract (GCE) Tap for how it works →"], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "How it works Green beans soak in hot water, which pulls out both caffeine and flavour compounds. That water — now a flavour-saturated \"green coffee extract\" — passes through an activated carbon filter that traps only the caffeine molecules. A fresh batch of beans is then soaked in this already flavour-saturated water: "], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Know this: no chemical solvents are involved at any stage, which makes it a favourite for organic-certified decaf. CO₂ Process Supercritical carbon dioxide"], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Uses: Pressurised CO₂ as the solvent Tap for how it works → How it works Moistened green beans sit in a sealed chamber with CO₂ pressurised into a \"supercritical\" state — dense like a liquid, but flowing like a gas. In that state it selectively bonds to caffeine molecules without pulling out much flavour. The pressure "], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Know this: favoured for large-scale commercial decaf since it preserves flavour well and leaves no chemical residue. Solvent-Based Process Direct or indirect method"], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Uses: Methylene chloride or ethyl acetate Tap for how it works → How it works In the direct method, steamed green beans are rinsed repeatedly with a solvent that bonds to caffeine, then steamed again to remove any trace of it. In the indirect method, beans are soaked in hot water first; that water is treated with the s"], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Know this: residual solvent levels are tightly regulated and tested to stay within food-safety limits well below any risk threshold. What decaffeination actually changes"], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Flavour Swiss Water and CO₂ methods tend to preserve origin character closely; solvent-based methods can shift aroma and flavour slightly more. Caffeine, not gone entirely"], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Decaf isn't caffeine-free — it typically still carries about 2–12mg per cup, versus roughly 95mg in a regular cup of drip coffee. Antioxidants stay mostly intact"], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Chlorogenic acids and other antioxidants aren't targeted by any decaffeination process, so decaf keeps most of coffee's other health-associated compounds."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Who reaches for it A common choice for pregnancy, caffeine sensitivity, sleep concerns, or simply an evening cup — without giving up the ritual of coffee itself."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "KEEP IT FRESH Storage & freshness notes Grind right before brewing Ground coffee oxidises within minutes; whole beans hold their flavour far longer than pre-ground coffee."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Airtight, dark, cool Store beans away from heat, light and moisture — an opaque, sealed container beats a clear jar on the counter. Skip the fridge Condensation from temperature swings does more harm than good — save the freezer only for long-term, tightly sealed portions."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Use within 2–4 weeks of roasting Look for a \"roasted on\" date, not just \"best by\" — freshly roasted beans also need a few days to rest and release CO₂ before brewing."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "SPEAK THE LANGUAGE Glossary of coffee terms Tap a term to reveal its definition. Crema The golden-brown foam on top of espresso, formed from emulsified oils and CO₂ under pressure."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Extraction The process of dissolving flavour compounds from grounds into water. Under-extracted tastes sour and weak; over-extracted tastes bitter and harsh."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "TDS Total Dissolved Solids — a measure of coffee concentration, used by baristas to dial in a brew's strength precisely. Single-origin Coffee from one country, region or farm, kept unblended so its specific character comes through."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Blend A mix of beans from different origins or roast levels, combined for a consistent, balanced flavour. Third Wave The modern specialty coffee movement that treats coffee like wine — focused on origin, processing and precise brewing."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Cupping A standardised tasting and scoring method professionals use to evaluate coffee quality. Green Coffee Unroasted coffee beans, still pale green, before any roasting has taken place."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Bloom The initial release of CO₂ and swelling of grounds the moment hot water first touches fresh coffee. Decaf Coffee with roughly 97%+ of its caffeine removed, typically via the Swiss Water, CO₂, or solvent-based process."], ["Coffee", "guides/coffee.html", "Compiled by Kamal Kant Gautam A complete reference guide to the world of coffee — origin, processing, roasting, brewing, culture and care. ← Flavour IQ"]];